          "heading": "2. The Creator–Creature Distinction",
          "html": "<p>At the foundation of Christian theology lies a distinction that shapes everything: <strong>God alone is uncreated</strong>. Every other being — human, angelic, material — exists because God made it and continues to uphold it. God exists in a category entirely His own.</p>\n<p>This has two immediate consequences for how we know God:</p>\n<p><strong>We know God analogically, not univocally.</strong> Our language about God is real and meaningful, but it does not work in exactly the same way as language about creatures. When we say God is \"good\" or \"wise,\" we are using words that overlap with our creaturely experience — but God's goodness and wisdom are infinite, undeived, and entirely unlike ours in degree and kind. The words connect; the realities diverge in ways we cannot fully trace.</p>\n<p><strong>We know God only because He stoops to us.</strong> DeYoung explains in <em>Daily Doctrine</em> (Day 22) that all genuine knowledge of God is a gift of divine accommodation — God bending to our level, speaking our language, revealing Himself in forms we can receive. No one climbs to God through superior intelligence or spiritual effort. Knowledge of God begins when God speaks.</p>",
          "plainText": "At the foundation of Christian theology lies a distinction that shapes everything: God alone is uncreated. Every other being — human, angelic, material — exists because God made it and continues to uphold it. God exists in a category entirely His own.\n\nThis has two immediate consequences for how we know God:\n\nWe know God analogically, not univocally. Our language about God is real and meaningful, but it does not work in exactly the same way as language about creatures. When we say God is \"good\" or \"wise,\" we are using words that overlap with our creaturely experience — but God's goodness and wisdom are infinite, undeived, and entirely unlike ours in degree and kind. The words connect; the realities diverge in ways we cannot fully trace.\n\nWe know God only because He stoops to us. DeYoung explains in Daily Doctrine (Day 22) that all genuine knowledge of God is a gift of divine accommodation — God bending to our level, speaking our language, revealing Himself in forms we can receive. No one climbs to God through superior intelligence or spiritual effort. Knowledge of God begins when God speaks."
