          "heading": "3. Classical Arguments for God's Existence",
          "html": "<p>Christian thinkers have long used philosophical arguments for God's existence. Charnock addresses these at length in his <em>Discourse on God's Existence</em>, treating Psalm 14:1 (\"The fool has said in his heart, 'There is no God'\") as his text. His point: the denial of God is not a conclusion that follows from evidence — it is a condition of the heart, a willful suppression of what creation plainly declares.</p>\n<p>The classical arguments are worth knowing, not as substitutes for faith, but as tools for conversation:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>The Cosmological Argument</strong>: Every effect has a cause. The chain of contingent causes cannot regress infinitely. There must be a self-existent, uncaused First Cause. Christians name this being the God of Scripture.</li><li><strong>The Teleological Argument</strong>: The ordered complexity of creation — from the structure of DNA to the fine-tuning of physical constants — points to purposeful design. Romans 1:20 confirms that God's eternal power and divine nature are \"clearly perceived\" through what He has made.</li><li><strong>The Ontological Argument</strong>: The very concept of God as \"that than which nothing greater can be conceived\" implies that He must exist in reality, not merely in thought — since a being that truly exists is greater than one that exists only as a concept.</li></ul>\n<p>Charnock's application is pastoral: these arguments leave the unbeliever \"without excuse.\" They do not produce saving faith on their own, but they expose the irrationality of atheism. In the Philippine context, use them as <strong>conversation openers</strong> — not to win arguments, but to show that faith is not a retreat from reason.</p>",
          "plainText": "Christian thinkers have long used philosophical arguments for God's existence. Charnock addresses these at length in his Discourse on God's Existence, treating Psalm 14:1 (\"The fool has said in his heart, 'There is no God'\") as his text. His point: the denial of God is not a conclusion that follows from evidence — it is a condition of the heart, a willful suppression of what creation plainly declares.\n\nThe classical arguments are worth knowing, not as substitutes for faith, but as tools for conversation:\n\n- The Cosmological Argument: Every effect has a cause. The chain of contingent causes cannot regress infinitely. There must be a self-existent, uncaused First Cause. Christians name this being the God of Scripture.\n- The Teleological Argument: The ordered complexity of creation — from the structure of DNA to the fine-tuning of physical constants — points to purposeful design. Romans 1:20 confirms that God's eternal power and divine nature are \"clearly perceived\" through what He has made.\n- The Ontological Argument: The very concept of God as \"that than which nothing greater can be conceived\" implies that He must exist in reality, not merely in thought — since a being that truly exists is greater than one that exists only as a concept.\n\nCharnock's application is pastoral: these arguments leave the unbeliever \"without excuse.\" They do not produce saving faith on their own, but they expose the irrationality of atheism. In the Philippine context, use them as conversation openers — not to win arguments, but to show that faith is not a retreat from reason."
