          "html": "<div class=\"detail-table-wrap\"><table class=\"detail-table\"><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Core Point</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Knowability</td><td>We know God truly but not totally — always by His gracious accommodation</td></tr><tr><td>Creator–creature distinction</td><td>God alone is uncreated; our knowledge of Him is analogical and received</td></tr><tr><td>Classical arguments</td><td>Pointers to God's existence; leave the unbeliever without excuse (Rom. 1:20)</td></tr><tr><td>Aseity</td><td>God is self-existent and self-sufficient — He needs nothing, owes nothing, lacks nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Revelation</td><td>General (creation/conscience) gives context; Special (Scripture/Christ) gives the gospel</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>## 📚 EXTRA READING — Personal Library</p>\n<p><strong>Assigned for this session:</strong></p>\n<ul><li><em>Knowing God</em> — J.I. Packer, Chapters 1–3: \"The Study of God,\" \"The People Who Know Their God,\" \"Knowing and Being Known\"</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Reflection prompt:</strong> Write a one-paragraph description of God as you understood Him <em>before</em> this study. How does Packer's account challenge or confirm that picture?</p>\n<p><strong>Memory verse:</strong> Isaiah 55:8–9</p>\n<p>## 🔬 LAB / RESEARCH</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Field Interview:</strong> Ask two people (one Christian, one not): <em>\"If you could describe God in three words, what would they be?\"</em> Record and analyze their answers. What do they reveal about popular Filipino views of God? How do they align with or diverge from Scripture?</li></ol>\n<ol><li><strong>Folk Religion Analysis:</strong> Identify one popular Filipino belief about God — from folk Catholicism, prosperity preaching, or folk spirituality — and contrast it with the doctrine of aseity. Write 150 words.</li></ol>\n<ol><li><strong>Personal Reflection:</strong> Write 200 words: <em>What does it mean for your daily life that God is completely self-sufficient and needs nothing from you?</em></li></ol>",
          "plainText": "| Concept | Core Point |\n|---------|-----------|\n| Knowability | We know God truly but not totally — always by His gracious accommodation |\n| Creator–creature distinction | God alone is uncreated; our knowledge of Him is analogical and received |\n| Classical arguments | Pointers to God's existence; leave the unbeliever without excuse (Rom. 1:20) |\n| Aseity | God is self-existent and self-sufficient — He needs nothing, owes nothing, lacks nothing |\n| Revelation | General (creation/conscience) gives context; Special (Scripture/Christ) gives the gospel |\n\n\n## 📚 EXTRA READING — Personal Library\n\nAssigned for this session:\n- Knowing God — J.I. Packer, Chapters 1–3: \"The Study of God,\" \"The People Who Know Their God,\" \"Knowing and Being Known\"\n\nReflection prompt: Write a one-paragraph description of God as you understood Him before this study. How does Packer's account challenge or confirm that picture?\n\nMemory verse: Isaiah 55:8–9\n\n\n## 🔬 LAB / RESEARCH\n\n1. Field Interview: Ask two people (one Christian, one not): \"If you could describe God in three words, what would they be?\" Record and analyze their answers. What do they reveal about popular Filipino views of God? How do they align with or diverge from Scripture?\n\n2. Folk Religion Analysis: Identify one popular Filipino belief about God — from folk Catholicism, prosperity preaching, or folk spirituality — and contrast it with the doctrine of aseity. Write 150 words.\n\n3. Personal Reflection: Write 200 words: *What does it mean for your daily life that God is completely self-sufficient and needs nothing from you?"
