          "heading": "3. The Incommunicable Attributes",
          "html": "<p>DeYoung introduces the distinction in <em>Daily Doctrine</em> (Day 28): communicable attributes are those God shares in derivative form with His creatures — love, wisdom, knowledge. Incommunicable attributes belong to God alone, with no true creaturely analogy. These define His infinite greatness.</p>\n<p><strong>Omniscience</strong> — God knows all things past, present, and future simultaneously and completely. Jones explains in <em>God Is</em> (Ch. 9) that God's knowledge does not work like ours — sequential, dependent on input, capable of error. God knows all things \"in an eternal instant,\" seeing all times as equally present to Him. He has foreknowledge because He has foreordained; He declared the end from the beginning (Isa. 46:10) because He governs what will come to pass.</p>\n<p><strong>Omnipotence</strong> — God can do everything consistent with His nature and will. Jones treats this in <em>God Is</em> (Ch. 10) alongside His dominion: power is His ability to act; dominion is His sovereign right to act. Quoting Charnock, Jones notes that to imagine infinite power without sovereign dominion \"is to fancy a mighty senseless statue, fit to be beheld, but not fit to be obeyed.\" God's power is not raw force — it is the purposeful power of the infinitely wise and good God.</p>\n<p><strong>Omnipresence</strong> — God is fully and wholly present in every point of creation simultaneously. Jones draws on Charnock's threefold distinction in <em>God Is</em> (Ch. 8): physical bodies exist <em>circumscriptively</em> (bounded by space); finite spirits exist <em>definitively</em> (located but not spread); God exists <em>repletively</em> — He fills all things without being contained by anything. As Charnock put it: He is \"from the height of the heavens to the bottom of the deeps… yet not limited by it, but beyond it.\" Jones applies this practically: our sin is never hidden from Him; His comfort is never far from us.</p>\n<p><strong>Immutability</strong> — God does not change. Charnock's <em>Discourse on the Immutability of God</em> is one of the most pastorally rich sections of his work. DeYoung summarises the point in <em>Daily Doctrine</em> (Day 34): immutability means God's character, purposes, and promises are eternally stable. He does not improve or deteriorate, react or regret (in the ultimate sense). Malachi 3:6 — <em>\"I the LORD do not change\"</em> — is the anchor of every covenant promise. His people have always depended on this.</p>\n<p><strong>Eternality</strong> — God exists outside time. Charnock's <em>Discourse on the Eternity of God</em> explores what it means that God is not <em>old</em> but <em>eternal</em> — time is a creature; God is its Creator and Lord. All moments are equally present to His eternal mind. He does not react to history; He governs it from above it. DeYoung (Day 35) notes that this makes God's foreknowledge and His decrees coherent: He ordains what comes to pass precisely because He stands above the sequence of events we call history.</p>",
          "plainText": "DeYoung introduces the distinction in Daily Doctrine (Day 28): communicable attributes are those God shares in derivative form with His creatures — love, wisdom, knowledge. Incommunicable attributes belong to God alone, with no true creaturely analogy. These define His infinite greatness.\n\nOmniscience — God knows all things past, present, and future simultaneously and completely. Jones explains in God Is (Ch. 9) that God's knowledge does not work like ours — sequential, dependent on input, capable of error. God knows all things \"in an eternal instant,\" seeing all times as equally present to Him. He has foreknowledge because He has foreordained; He declared the end from the beginning (Isa. 46:10) because He governs what will come to pass.\n\nOmnipotence — God can do everything consistent with His nature and will. Jones treats this in God Is (Ch. 10) alongside His dominion: power is His ability to act; dominion is His sovereign right to act. Quoting Charnock, Jones notes that to imagine infinite power without sovereign dominion \"is to fancy a mighty senseless statue, fit to be beheld, but not fit to be obeyed.\" God's power is not raw force — it is the purposeful power of the infinitely wise and good God.\n\nOmnipresence — God is fully and wholly present in every point of creation simultaneously. Jones draws on Charnock's threefold distinction in God Is (Ch. 8): physical bodies exist circumscriptively (bounded by space); finite spirits exist definitively (located but not spread); God exists repletively — He fills all things without being contained by anything. As Charnock put it: He is \"from the height of the heavens to the bottom of the deeps… yet not limited by it, but beyond it.\" Jones applies this practically: our sin is never hidden from Him; His comfort is never far from us.\n\nImmutability — God does not change. Charnock's Discourse on the Immutability of God is one of the most pastorally rich sections of his work. DeYoung summarises the point in Daily Doctrine (Day 34): immutability means God's character, purposes, and promises are eternally stable. He does not improve or deteriorate, react or regret (in the ultimate sense). Malachi 3:6 — \"I the LORD do not change\" — is the anchor of every covenant promise. His people have always depended on this.\n\nEternality — God exists outside time. Charnock's Discourse on the Eternity of God explores what it means that God is not old but eternal — time is a creature; God is its Creator and Lord. All moments are equally present to His eternal mind. He does not react to history; He governs it from above it. DeYoung (Day 35) notes that this makes God's foreknowledge and His decrees coherent: He ordains what comes to pass precisely because He stands above the sequence of events we call history."
