          "heading": "1. Defining Sovereignty",
          "html": "<p>God's sovereignty is His absolute right and unrivalled power to rule over all creation according to His own will and purposes. He is not a constitutional monarch — not a ruler whose authority depends on the consent of the governed. He is the supreme King whose dominion is original, total, and unassailable.</p>\n<p>Charnock's <em>Discourse on God's Dominion</em> (Discourse 13) distinguishes between God's power and His dominion: His power is His ability to act; His dominion is His royal prerogative — His sovereign right to act as He sees fit over all that exists. Quoting Charnock: \"It is as possible for him not to be God as not to be supreme.\" Dominion is not an optional add-on to God's nature; it is inseparable from who He is.</p>\n<p>Mark Jones emphasises in <em>God Is</em> (Ch. 15) that God's sovereignty is not mere raw force. It is the dominion of a God whose will is intrinsically righteous, wise, and good. This means His sovereignty cannot be tyrannical — it is the expression of infinite goodness exercised with infinite power. For the believer, this is not a doctrine to fear but a reality to rest in.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"detail-quote\"><p>\"From him and through him and to him are all things. To him be glory forever.\" — Romans 11:36</p></blockquote>",
          "plainText": "God's sovereignty is His absolute right and unrivalled power to rule over all creation according to His own will and purposes. He is not a constitutional monarch — not a ruler whose authority depends on the consent of the governed. He is the supreme King whose dominion is original, total, and unassailable.\n\nCharnock's Discourse on God's Dominion (Discourse 13) distinguishes between God's power and His dominion: His power is His ability to act; His dominion is His royal prerogative — His sovereign right to act as He sees fit over all that exists. Quoting Charnock: \"It is as possible for him not to be God as not to be supreme.\" Dominion is not an optional add-on to God's nature; it is inseparable from who He is.\n\nMark Jones emphasises in God Is (Ch. 15) that God's sovereignty is not mere raw force. It is the dominion of a God whose will is intrinsically righteous, wise, and good. This means His sovereignty cannot be tyrannical — it is the expression of infinite goodness exercised with infinite power. For the believer, this is not a doctrine to fear but a reality to rest in.\n\n> \"From him and through him and to him are all things. To him be glory forever.\" — Romans 11:36"
