          "heading": "6. Summary",
          "html": "<div class=\"detail-table-wrap\"><table class=\"detail-table\"><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Core Point (from Crossway sources)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sovereignty</td><td>God's absolute right and power to rule all things; inseparable from who He is (Charnock)</td></tr><tr><td>Preservation</td><td>God upholds all things in existence moment by moment (DeYoung)</td></tr><tr><td>Concurrence</td><td>God works through secondary causes; He is not the author of evil (Jones/DeYoung)</td></tr><tr><td>Government</td><td>God directs all events toward His purposes — nothing escapes His hand (DeYoung)</td></tr><tr><td>Prayer</td><td>Ordained as a real means through which God acts; sovereignty fuels, not undermines it (DeYoung)</td></tr><tr><td>Evangelism</td><td>God's sovereignty is the ground of evangelistic confidence, not its enemy (DeYoung)</td></tr><tr><td>Suffering</td><td>The answer is a person — the God who governs all things and pledges our ultimate good (Jones)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>## 📚 EXTRA READING — Personal Library</p>\n<p><strong>Assigned for this session:</strong></p>\n<ul><li><em>None Greater</em> — Matthew Barrett, Chapters 6–7: \"The God Who Is in Control\"</li><li><em>God's Glory Alone</em> — David VanDrunen, Chapter 4</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Reflection prompt:</strong> Describe a situation in your life where you struggled to trust God's sovereignty. How does this session's teaching speak to that situation?</p>\n<p>## 🔬 LAB / RESEARCH</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Scripture Search:</strong> Find three passages in Paul's letters where he connects God's sovereignty to his own motivation for ministry or endurance in suffering. Write a one-page summary of what you find.</li></ol>\n<ol><li><strong>Case Study:</strong> Read Acts 4:23–31. How does the early church use the doctrine of God's sovereignty as the foundation for bold prayer and courageous mission? Write 200 words applying this to your local church context.</li></ol>\n<ol><li><strong>Sovereignty Journal:</strong> For one week, note one event per day and reflect on how God's sovereign hand may be at work in it. Write a short paragraph each day. At the end of the week, review what you wrote: What changed in how you prayed or responded?</li></ol>",
          "plainText": "| Concept | Core Point (from Crossway sources) |\n|---------|----------------------------------|\n| Sovereignty | God's absolute right and power to rule all things; inseparable from who He is (Charnock) |\n| Preservation | God upholds all things in existence moment by moment (DeYoung) |\n| Concurrence | God works through secondary causes; He is not the author of evil (Jones/DeYoung) |\n| Government | God directs all events toward His purposes — nothing escapes His hand (DeYoung) |\n| Prayer | Ordained as a real means through which God acts; sovereignty fuels, not undermines it (DeYoung) |\n| Evangelism | God's sovereignty is the ground of evangelistic confidence, not its enemy (DeYoung) |\n| Suffering | The answer is a person — the God who governs all things and pledges our ultimate good (Jones) |\n\n\n## 📚 EXTRA READING — Personal Library\n\nAssigned for this session:\n- None Greater — Matthew Barrett, Chapters 6–7: \"The God Who Is in Control\"\n- God's Glory Alone — David VanDrunen, Chapter 4\n\nReflection prompt: Describe a situation in your life where you struggled to trust God's sovereignty. How does this session's teaching speak to that situation?\n\n\n## 🔬 LAB / RESEARCH\n\n1. Scripture Search: Find three passages in Paul's letters where he connects God's sovereignty to his own motivation for ministry or endurance in suffering. Write a one-page summary of what you find.\n\n2. Case Study: Read Acts 4:23–31. How does the early church use the doctrine of God's sovereignty as the foundation for bold prayer and courageous mission? Write 200 words applying this to your local church context.\n\n3. Sovereignty Journal: For one week, note one event per day and reflect on how God's sovereign hand may be at work in it. Write a short paragraph each day. At the end of the week, review what you wrote: What changed in how you prayed or responded?"
